      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hmm, I think that they may be partially overlapping, but this should still be useful. The documentation for the NetworkOfferCallback mentions:\n```\nWhen an offer is no longer useful, the stack will inform the provider by calling {@link NetworkOfferCallback#onNetworkUnneeded}. The provider should stop trying to bring up such a network, or disconnect it if it already has one.\n```\n\nFWIU, NetworkOfferCallback#onNetworkUnneeded acts on the NetworkOffer (it is what calls NetworkFactory#releaseNetworkFor) and ConnectivityService#teardownUnneededNetwork acts on the NetworkAgent. There may not always be a registered NetworkAgent when onNetworkUnneeded is called (one may be in the process of being created).\n\nIn any case, this should be the safer variant and exactly what VcnNetworkProvider does (the one other direct user of the NetworkProvider / NetworkOffer API).",
